@PACKAGE_INIT@

# Package config input for configure_package_config_file() — see
# cmake/install.cmake. This is what a consumer's find_package(<project> CONFIG)
# ends up executing.
#
# Nothing here spells the project name literally: @PROJECT_NAME@ is substituted
# at configure time, and the project name comes from the directory name. A fork
# copies this file verbatim.

# ── Refuse a config with no targets beside it ─────────────────────────────────
# This file can be found in two places where it does not describe a usable
# package, and in both the failure without this guard is "include could not find
# requested file" naming a path the consumer never wrote — which reads like a
# broken package rather than the wrong prefix or the wrong build options.
#
#   A build directory on CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH. This file is generated into the
#   build tree and installed from there, so find_package finds it, but no
#   Targets file is generated beside it: this project exports at install time
#   only (cmake/install.cmake explains why there is no build-tree export).
#
#   An install prefix built with the library switched off. Then there was no
#   target to export, and the package config was installed anyway.
#
# Reporting it this way rather than with FATAL_ERROR is what lets find_package
# say "considered this prefix and rejected it, here is why", and keeps a
# non-REQUIRED find_package optional, as its caller asked. It is the same
# mechanism CMake's own generated Targets files use to report a dependency they
# could not resolve.
#
# The message is deliberately one quoted argument: set() with several would
# build a ;-separated list and print as one run-on line.
#
# Both paths are exercised by ctest `consumer-resolves` (GitHub #12). It points
# one generated consumer at the build tree and another at an install configured
# with @PROJECT_NAME@_BUILD_LIB=OFF, and requires this message rather than merely
# a nonzero configure result. Deleting this block falls through to `include`
# below and still fails, so an exit-code-only check would be vacuous.
if (NOT EXISTS "${CMAKE_CURRENT_LIST_DIR}/@PROJECT_NAME@Targets.cmake")
  set(@PROJECT_NAME@_FOUND FALSE)
  set(@PROJECT_NAME@_NOT_FOUND_MESSAGE
      "${CMAKE_CURRENT_LIST_DIR} holds a package config but no exported targets beside it. Either it is a build tree rather than an install prefix — this project exports at install time only, so run `cmake --install` and point CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH at the prefix, or consume it with add_subdirectory() or FetchContent — or it was installed with the library disabled, and there was nothing to export.")
  return()
endif ()

# ── Dependencies this project's exported targets refer to ─────────────────────
# Every dependency named in the installed Targets file has to be found again
# here, or the consumer gets "The following imported targets are referenced, but
# are missing: <dep>" — which reads like a broken package rather than a missing
# find_package.
#
# The rule is *not* "public dependencies only", which is easy to assume and
# wrong. Anything linked into an exported target reaches the Targets file: a
# PUBLIC or INTERFACE link appears directly, and a PRIVATE link into a static
# library appears wrapped in $<LINK_ONLY:...>, because a consumer still has to
# link it. CMake checks both. Only dependencies of something that is never
# exported — the executable, the tests — can be left out.
#
# The other half of that rule lives in cmake/deps/: a dependency that reaches
# the Targets file must also have been installed and exported by *its* build, or
# install(EXPORT) cannot resolve it and this package is never produced at all.
# See the install-visibility note in cmake/deps/catch2.cmake.
#
# Use find_dependency, not find_package: it forwards REQUIRED/QUIET and reports
# a miss as "package X not found because dependency Y was missing".
#
# termforge is linked PUBLIC into glyphcade_core — the bottom of src/lib's link
# chain, so every target above it inherits termforge through the PUBLIC closure,
# glyphcade_lib included (the Shell derives from termforge::App and names its
# types in our headers). An installed glyphcadeTargets.cmake therefore references
# termforge::lib, and this line is what lets a consumer resolve it.
#
# ⚠ termforge::lib is the ONLY external name any of our four exported targets
# references, which is what keeps this file to a single find_dependency. Adding
# an external link to any of them adds a line here — and if it is rtaudio,
# cmake/check_export.cmake goes red first.
#
# ⚠ 0.6.0, at patch granularity, and it must track the floor in
# cmake/deps/termforge.cmake exactly. This line used to say 0.1 while that file
# said 0.1.10 — with a comment claiming the two matched, which they did not.
#
# The reason to align them is stronger than symmetry, because termforge's
# version file is SameMinorVersion and "0.6" therefore accepts any 0.6.x. The
# Shell holds a termforge::ListWidget BY VALUE, in a header we install; v0.1.11
# added members to that class, and v0.2.1 did it AGAIN (the scrollbar's track
# and thumb colours, #21). So a consumer resolving an older 0.6.x compiles our
# public header against one object layout while glyphcade_lib was built against
# another. Nothing fails to find, nothing fails to link — the sizes just
# disagree. Anything we hold by value in an installed header makes the build
# floor an ABI floor too, and this is the line that enforces it consumer-side.
# Two size changes in two minor versions is the base rate, not bad luck.
#
# ⚠ term-game#36 makes it three, and the third is the one this comment would have
# missed. v0.4.0 rebuilt Button's press flash as a duration pair (#69), so
# sizeof(Button) grew — and Shell holds no Button. It holds a ConfirmDialog,
# which holds two. So sizeof(Shell) moved through a class our headers never
# name, and the same tag pair inserted two virtuals into Widget's vtable AHEAD
# of six existing slots. Auditing "which termforge types does shell.hpp name" is
# not enough to know whether this floor needs raising; transitive members and
# vtable layout both count, and neither is visible from our side.
#
# ⚠ Crossing a minor is a one-way door for consumers: SameMinorVersion means
# 0.6.0 no longer accepts any 0.2.x (intended — the ABI differs, above), and
# equally that a stale 0.2.2 request stops matching a 0.6.x install. That is
# why this line and cmake/deps/termforge.cmake move in the same commit; a
# half-done bump produces a package that resolves in our tree and nowhere else.
#
# This line IS exercised, as of term-game#46 — but read what by, because the two
# checks are easy to confuse. cmake/check_export.cmake installs to a scratch
# prefix and greps the generated targets file as text; it never calls
# find_package, and a wrong version here stays invisible to it. The check that
# catches this line is its sibling cmake/check_consumer.cmake, ctest name
# **consumer-resolves**, which configures a generated throwaway consumer against
# a scratch install and so actually EXECUTES the find_dependency below.
#
# Demonstrated rather than assumed: with 0.2.2 here against a 0.6.0 install,
# consumer-resolves goes red with "Could not find a configuration file for
# package termforge" while check_export still reports "export is rtaudio-free:
# CLEAN". That contrast is why #46 existed.
#
# ⚠ One thing it cannot catch, so keep this line and the pin in
# cmake/deps/termforge.cmake in the same commit anyway: it compares the floor
# against the version that actually resolved, and under SameMinorVersion a
# successful resolution already implies those agree. Its independent teeth are
# on the line being deleted or stripped of its version, which resolution can
# never notice because it then always succeeds.
include(CMakeFindDependencyMacro)
find_dependency(termforge 0.6.0)

include("${CMAKE_CURRENT_LIST_DIR}/@PROJECT_NAME@Targets.cmake")

check_required_components(@PROJECT_NAME@)
